<%# Page header - title, optional description, optional actions on the right.

    design.md scale: page title is text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight, body is
    text-sm. Airbnb reading: the title owns the top of the page with real
    breathing room beneath it, and actions sit on the same optical line.

    This renders the page's single h1, so it declares :own_heading. The admin layout
    reads that and steps its own visually hidden h1 aside; without it an admin page
    using this partial would carry two h1s. The application layout does not read it,
    so declaring it is harmless elsewhere.

    Locals:
      title       - page title (renders the single h1)
      description - optional supporting sentence
      badge       - optional status pill, rendered on the title's line
      actions     - optional block of buttons/links, passed as a block

    `badge` exists because the block is the *actions* slot and a status is not an action. The grade
    book put its Draft pill there and it floated in the top-right corner among where "Add student"
    and "Edit classroom" live - the same fault as the trading switch in a page header, one page over.
    An entity's state belongs beside its name: Linear, Stripe and GitHub all put it there. %>
<% content_for :own_heading, true %>
<%# mb-6 and nothing else. The pb-5 that used to sit here existed to hold content off
    the rule beneath the title; with the rule gone it was surplus, stacking 20px of
    padding on top of 24px of margin. design.md's page rhythm is a 24px header block.

    items-end when the title stands alone, items-start when it carries a subtitle - design.md
    is explicit about this and records it as a bug it had already measured once. A 40px CTA in
    a row with a 32px h1 makes the block 40px tall; with items-start the title sits at the top
    and leaves 8px of dead space beneath it, so the gap to the content below measured 32px
    instead of the intended 24px. items-end puts the CTA on the h1's baseline and closes it.
    With a subtitle the reverse applies: items-end would drop the CTA to the subtitle. %>
<% align = local_assigns[:description].present? ? "lg:items-start" : "lg:items-end" %>
<div class="mb-6 flex flex-col gap-3 lg:flex-row <%= align %> lg:justify-between">
  <div class="min-w-0">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      <%# `min-w-0 break-words`, and it needs both. A title is one long word often enough -
        "Announcements" at 200% text is 346px against a 305px viewport - and a word that cannot break
        pushes the whole page sideways.

        `break-words` alone did nothing here, which is worth knowing: this h1 is a **flex item** (the
        row it sits in also holds the status badge), so its `min-width: auto` floors it at min-content,
        and the min-content of a single unbreakable word is the whole word. `min-w-0` removes the floor;
        only then does `overflow-wrap` get to break it. %>
      <h1 class="min-w-0 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight break-words text-slate-900"><%= title %></h1>
      <%= local_assigns[:badge] %>
    </div>
    <% if local_assigns[:description].present? %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600"><%= description %></p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# Always rendered with `layout:`, so yield is available. An empty block just
      yields blank, which is why this checks content rather than block_given? -
      that is unreliable inside a partial layout. %>
  <% actions = yield %>
  <% if actions.present? %>
    <div class="flex shrink-0 flex-wrap items-center gap-2"><%= actions %></div>
  <% end %>
</div>
